'''Vietnamese Baliscans''' ({{Langx|bl|Baliscanos vietnamitas}}; {{langx|vi|Người Balisca gốc Việt}}) are [[Baliscans|Baliscan citizens]] of full or partial [[w:Vietnam|Vietnamese]] ancestry. They constitute one of the oldest, largest, and most integrated Asian communities within the [[Baliscan Confederation]]. According to the [[2025 Baliscan census]], there were 1,248,735 individuals identifying as Vietnamese Baliscan, inclusive of those identifying with multiple ethnicities.

While limited ties existed during the [[w:French Indochina|French colonial period]] through Balisca's Francophone regions ([[Maracaju]] and [[Catanduva]]), the community's primary demographic foundation was laid during the massive state-sponsored resettlement of refugees following the end of the [[w:Vietnam War|Vietnam War]] in 1975. Due to Balisca's geopolitical stance of "active non-alignment" and its historical ideological sympathy with anti-colonial movements, the Confederation became a primary destination for Indochinese refugees in the Western Hemisphere.

